module bindings.kotlin.openSearchTenantScopedDocumentAccess;

// OpenSearch's security plugin gives every user a private tenant, and it
// addresses all of them by one reserved name: `__user__`. That is what
// `org.opensearch.commons.authuser.User.getRequestedTenant()` returns for any
// principal browsing their private space, so the tenant name is identical for
// every user in the cluster and comparing it against a stored document's
// tenant separates one user's private documents from another's not at all.
// The security plugin's own documentation states the consequence -- private
// tenant content is never shared -- and the plugins that persist tenant-scoped
// documents implement it by adding the requester's own name to the document's
// access list and matching on that.
//
// The OpenSearch plugins that store such documents share one class for it,
// `UserAccessManager`, replicated in observability, reporting and
// simple-schema. It has two halves that have to agree: `getSearchAccessInfo`
// builds the filter a search runs under and returns `listOf("User:${user.name}")`
// for a private-tenant requester, and `doesUserHasAccess(user, tenant, access)`
// is the per-document decision the get/update/delete paths call. Both read the
// requester's tenant through `getUserTenant`, and `isUserPrivateTenant` is the
// contract's name for the `__user__` comparison.
//
// A `getUserTenant` read whose function then decides on the document's access
// list or on the requester's roles, without asking anywhere in that function
// whether the requester is in their private tenant, is the half that disagrees:
// tenant equality holds for every user of the cluster inside `__user__`, so the
// role or filter-by branch it falls through to hands one user's private
// documents to another (CWE-285, CWE-639). Whether that is a finding is
// VYQL-AUTH-081's call; this says only which decision omitted the constraint.
//
// Residual: the private-tenant question is recognised through the contract's
// `isUserPrivateTenant` predicate, which every copy of the class declares, so a
// plugin that renames it or inlines the `__user__` comparison instead of
// calling it still flags. Kotlin emits no literal context tokens, so the
// sentinel itself is not readable from the decision's function scope.
binding kotlinTenantScopedDocumentAccessWithoutPrivateTenantConstraint {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "call" and node.path ~= "getUserTenant" and containsAny(node.context.scopeCall, ["access.contains", "backendRoles.map", "roles.stream", "roles.map"]) and not containsAny(node.context.scopeCall, ["isUserPrivateTenant"])
  emit issue code.IdorPolicyReview at node
}
